The one correction is: the green isn't usually the main protective board itself, and the lines aren't copper cables.

Think of it like this:
Yeah — you're basically describing a PCB (Printed Circuit Board), like the motherboard inside a PC, switch, graphics card, etc.
PCB
│
├── Fibreglass board → Gives it strength/structure
├── Copper traces    → Tiny electrical pathways
├── Solder mask      → Green protective coating
└── Components       → Chips, resistors, capacitors, etc.
🟩 The green stuff — Solder Mask

The green layer you see is called the solder mask.

It's a thin protective coating placed over the copper underneath. It helps protect the copper from things like oxidation and prevents accidental electrical connections during soldering.

It doesn't actually have to be green — PCBs can be black, blue, red, white, etc. Green is just extremely common.

Underneath that is usually a rigid material such as fibreglass/epoxy (FR-4) that gives the board its physical structure.

🟠 The little lines — Copper Traces

You're right that they're made from copper, but they're called traces, not cables.

A trace is basically a tiny flat electrical wire printed onto the PCB.

Chip A
  │
  └════════════════ Chip B
       copper trace

They can carry electrical signals/data, but traces can also carry power and ground.

So don't think:

Copper traces = data

Think:

Copper traces = electrical pathways.

What the electricity represents depends on the circuit.

🧠 And PCBs can have traces INSIDE them

This part is pretty cool. A motherboard isn't necessarily just copper lines on the top and bottom.

Modern PCBs can contain many internal copper layers:

Top components
────────────────
Copper layer
────────────────
Insulating material
────────────────
Copper layer
────────────────
Insulating material
────────────────
Copper layer
────────────────
Bottom components

Tiny plated holes called vias allow electrical connections to travel between those layers.

So when you look at a motherboard and see a trace disappear, it may not actually end — it can go through a via, travel along an internal layer, and come back out somewhere else.
